    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_f30572c2fdca0cd3973b62156f6be585f571536df415c55d27e4e18c5b5f18b1",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The infinite-phase limit remains explicit: the ordinary characteristic function is an absolutely convergent product of one-phase factors, while the derivative-weighted characteristic function is an absolutely convergent sum of product-weighted terms. This retains coordinate-level provenance in the limiting laws instead of replacing the finite models with opaque measures. The representation is justified only under the same square-summability conditions that control the products and companion series.",
