    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_31208b3fc3904c9d2dec695bf8db97f69e1d0cea8eaf37051ef9edfb260f22da",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Besicovitch mean convergence can preserve Jensen means even though log|f| is singular at zeros because analyticity supplies a local continuity substitute: nested rectangles turn global mean error into integrable maximum envelopes, noncollapse makes Jensen zero-count bounds available, and truncated logarithms isolate the singular set. Removing the truncation only after controlling approximation and height then transfers averaged logarithmic means and their motion/zero consequences; the later good/bad-box argument refines the same strategy into separately controlled tail, truncation, and approximation errors.",
