    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_f8cdf4d01798c550f3abdea26eca5a80dd8043f07fe54e4bc084f349d049eea9",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "A global logarithm is unnecessary when branch defects can be separated into exact finite-height topology and asymptotically sparse error. Horizontal cuts and sided continuation first make path variations well-defined; constant Jensen data for the underlying exponential then forces zero strip density, so cut-bearing boxes have mean density zero. Local Backlund-type argument bounds control the cost of each defect, loop terms exactly repair contour identities at finite height and vanish after averaging, and a good/bad-box characteristic-function estimate transfers the limiting log-value law.",
